waving flags and chanting slogans for  the third year protesters in hartoum  took to the streets to remember victims  of a brutal crackdown by security forces  to break up a pro-democracy sitting at  the army headquarters i want to see  fair trails  for all those who involved  the killers are known  not as  individual soldiers but including those  who planned  for this massacre  and those who ordered  for it  there were similar protests in other  cities the 2019 sit-in led to the ouster  of president omar al-bashir after 30  years in power but it continued as  demonstrators demanded a civilian  government  witnesses and survivors have accused the  security forces of using excessive force  to disperse protesters there were  reports of bodies being thrown into the  nile river and allegations of sexual  violence a transitional government sworn  in three months after the attack  promised to investigate the attack that  government was deposed last year  since the military took power in october  hopes have faded that justice will be  served anytime soon the committee set up  to investigate the attack was suspended  and nearly eight months on there's been  no talk of reviving it  amira caboose lost her 26 year old son  in the sit-in she says despite the  delays she continues to demand for those  responsible for his death to be held  accountable  there was a postponement in the issue of  justice for the protester killed and  people would say let's focus on moving  forward and the issue of justice would  come later this is something we will not  accept ever again the delay has led to  more lives lost in protests  the u.n mission in sudan along with the  african union and the regional body igad  are facilitating talks to end sudan's  political crisis resulting from the  military takeover it's due to launch  talks between the various sides next  week we are quite sure  that justice  for the victims  a victim-centered perspective of justice  and transitional justice  as protesters continue to call for  justice they're also asking for the  military to hand over power to a  civilian government nearly 100 people  have been killed in anti-military  demonstrations and the calls for  accountability are growing louder but  achieving it still remains far from  reach heber morgan al-jazeera horton
 

